WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.546 --> 00:00:12.675
Four hundred years ago, a village by the name
of Amsterdam set out to conquer the world,

00:00:12.687 --> 00:00:17.886
invented capitalism as we know it, and became
the richest city on the European continent.

00:00:18.174 --> 00:00:21.162
there was this sense among
the Dutch that whoever you were,

00:00:21.808 --> 00:00:26.171
you could have a piece of the action. You
could be involved in this great economic

00:00:26.867 --> 00:00:28.633
 activity of your society.

00:00:31.655 --> 00:00:35.645
One hundred years later,
London, its main rival, took its place.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.491
The greatest trading power in
the world, London endured terrific

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:49.227
hardships, but went on to
become the world's largest city,

00:00:50.025 --> 00:00:52.657
and invented the megalopolis
of tomorrow's world.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:56.477
When the railways crashed
through the poorer areas of London,

00:00:56.901 --> 00:01:00.534
people were just dispersed. They
were swept aside, you might say.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:08.820
Meanwhile, New Amsterdam had now
become New York. It was under British rule.

00:01:09.312 --> 00:01:11.943
The city cut itself free
and entered the fray.

00:01:13.879 --> 00:01:17.519
At the outpost of the technical
revolution, New York became

00:01:17.531 --> 00:01:21.366
the vertical city, and created
a whole new horizon for itself.

00:01:21.366 --> 00:01:25.282
You watched day by
day as this stone structure

00:01:25.294 --> 00:01:28.972
was being built in
the middle of the river.

00:01:29.838 --> 00:01:30.653
 And it got bigger

00:01:31.146 --> 00:01:32.606
 and bigger and bigger.

00:01:53.743 --> 00:01:58.056
Today, we admire the beauty of the
city's houses, and the charm of its canals.

00:01:59.414 --> 00:02:01.587
But there's much more
to Amsterdam than that.

00:02:05.084 --> 00:02:07.682
It marks the starting
point of a great adventure.

00:02:08.921 --> 00:02:11.010
 That of the rise of liberal capitalism,

00:02:11.893 --> 00:02:13.913
 the expansion of worldwide commerce,

00:02:14.388 --> 00:02:16.290
 and the emergence of the global city.

00:02:19.312 --> 00:02:23.370
A combination of circumstances that
has rarely been seen in human history,

00:02:23.896 --> 00:02:26.290
and one which changed
the face of the world.

00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:40.414
Amsterdam was still just a small
town of less than thirty thousand

00:02:40.426 --> 00:02:44.320
inhabitants, a town of small consequence
in terms of the European continent.

00:02:47.258 --> 00:02:51.342
On this spring morning of 1594,
near Dam Square, which has been the

00:02:51.354 --> 00:02:55.390
heart of Amsterdam since its very
origins, ten men held a meeting.

00:03:00.585 --> 00:03:03.183
The meeting took place in
one of the participants' houses,

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.254
 a certain Martin Spill,

00:03:05.662 --> 00:03:07.003
 a brewer by profession.

00:03:14.015 --> 00:03:17.661
Around the table sat men whose
businesses traded wood, salt, and

00:03:17.673 --> 00:03:21.162
herring, products which Amsterdam
had become a specialist in.

00:03:23.675 --> 00:03:25.732
Their ships were,
however, excluded from the

00:03:25.744 --> 00:03:27.767
trade of pepper and
spices with South Asia,

00:03:28.616 --> 00:03:30.415
 the most lucrative products around.

00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:41.309
Because this form of trade was run by Portugal,
which owned a huge number of trading posts along

00:03:41.321 --> 00:03:46.324
the route to Asia, serving as stopovers for its
trading boats and as bases for its warships.

00:03:48.803 --> 00:03:51.036
On the day in question,
it was this very monopoly

00:03:51.048 --> 00:03:52.979
that was at the heart
of their discussion.

00:03:56.001 --> 00:03:59.363
And the idea is we can
attack the Portuguese

00:03:59.855 --> 00:04:02.147
 monopoly on trade with the Far East.

00:04:02.741 --> 00:04:06.578
And they realized that by this time,
the city of Amsterdam has developed.

00:04:07.173 --> 00:04:11.786
It's got money. It's got these people
with this kind of expertise. They've been

00:04:11.798 --> 00:04:16.307
building ships. So in other words, all
the ingredients are coming into place.

00:04:20.840 --> 00:04:24.558
Each of the men present that morning had
already amassed a fair amount of capital.

00:04:28.786 --> 00:04:31.558
But it was nothing compared
to what they'd soon own, and

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.354
the veritable empire they'd
contribute towards founding.

00:04:37.818 --> 00:04:42.147
Among them was Dirk van Oss,
one of the city's most dynamic traders.

00:04:44.354 --> 00:04:46.556
Originally from Antwerp,
he was a refugee,

00:04:46.568 --> 00:04:49.040
like many of the men
who attended this meeting.

00:04:52.028 --> 00:04:54.679
They moved to Amsterdam
following a conflict that had been

00:04:54.691 --> 00:04:57.444
ravaging this part of Europe
for the past twenty-five years.

00:05:03.998 --> 00:05:06.284
On one side was William
the Silent, who was

00:05:06.296 --> 00:05:08.803
championing the cause
of the Dutch Protestants.

00:05:12.911 --> 00:05:16.202
On the other side was Philip
II of Spain, an uncompromising

00:05:16.214 --> 00:05:19.516
Catholic and one of the most
powerful men on the continent.

00:05:23.047 --> 00:05:26.884
When the northern provinces
revolted, he put them to fire and sword.

00:05:29.244 --> 00:05:32.572
But Amsterdam and the Netherlands
finally gained their independence,

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:36.154
whereas Antwerp and Flanders
remained under his control.

00:05:40.823 --> 00:05:45.395
Antwerp was, at that point, the main
commercial city in Europe, the richest city

00:05:45.407 --> 00:05:49.991
in the continent, a primordial source
of taxation for Philip II and his kingdom.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:54.524
 However, the King of Spain made a bet,

00:05:55.186 --> 00:05:56.477
 which would cost him dearly.

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:00.993
 They knew that one way or another,

00:06:01.417 --> 00:06:03.658
 you couldn't lock up all the merchants.

00:06:04.202 --> 00:06:05.611
 Money is mobile.

00:06:06.578 --> 00:06:07.275
 So they

00:06:07.716 --> 00:06:13.675
perhaps uniquely said to the
inhabitants of Antwerp, there is a choice.

00:06:16.273 --> 00:06:19.367
You can either stay with
us, you have to convert

00:06:19.379 --> 00:06:22.232
to Catholicism if you're
not yet a Catholic,

00:06:22.843 --> 00:06:25.892
but you could also leave
and you have three years

00:06:25.904 --> 00:06:28.904
to liquidate your assets
and take them with you.

00:06:33.132 --> 00:06:35.385
Half of the inhabitants
of Antwerp, who were

00:06:35.397 --> 00:06:37.461
often more enterprising,
chose to leave.

00:06:40.415 --> 00:06:42.470
 Many of them left for London or Germany,

00:06:43.115 --> 00:06:45.531
but ultimately chose to
settle just two hundred

00:06:45.543 --> 00:06:48.174
kilometres from their city
of origin, in Amsterdam.

00:06:54.117 --> 00:06:57.631
The richest among them bought themselves
a house in the small medieval town.

00:06:59.753 --> 00:07:02.472
But those who couldn't
afford to do so built their own

00:07:02.484 --> 00:07:05.067
homes outside the city
walls, on agricultural land.

00:07:08.582 --> 00:07:11.332
There would soon be dozens
of thousands of people here,

00:07:11.842 --> 00:07:13.438
 waiting for a better solution.

00:07:21.587 --> 00:07:24.934
For these refugees, it was
the start of a new life, and when

00:07:24.946 --> 00:07:28.140
a chance presented itself,
they weren't going to miss it.

00:07:30.263 --> 00:07:33.913
They sit down with their cartographer.
They've got all the people there.

00:07:34.354 --> 00:07:36.642
And they work out a system
of how they're going to do

00:07:36.654 --> 00:07:39.210
this, a route they're going
to go, what ships they'll need.

00:07:39.753 --> 00:07:43.344
And they form a company, which
they call the Compagnie Fanfare,

00:07:43.356 --> 00:07:47.071
which translates as something
like the Company for Faraway Lands.

00:07:47.903 --> 00:07:51.044
It's fair to say that this small
group were well prepared.

00:07:53.624 --> 00:07:57.190
A man named Cornelius de
Houtman was sent to Lisbon in Portugal,

00:07:57.937 --> 00:07:59.787
 where he would work for several years,

00:08:00.653 --> 00:08:02.843
 and spy on the leading market players.

00:08:05.237 --> 00:08:06.477
 He knew what he was doing,

00:08:07.054 --> 00:08:09.774
and despite his lack of
experience, he was designated

00:08:09.786 --> 00:08:12.164
by the group as the
leader of this expedition.

00:08:16.171 --> 00:08:22.208
The total amount invested was three hundred thousand Dutch
guilders, an enormous amount. Even the richest traders

00:08:22.232 --> 00:08:28.593
in Amsterdam didn't have these kinds of sums available in
cash. A house in Amsterdam cost around five thousand Dutch

00:08:28.605 --> 00:08:34.890
guilders, and that would get you a very beautiful house. So
they needed to involve a lot of traders in order to raise

00:08:34.914 --> 00:08:41.146
such a sum of money. Certainly, dozens of traders were
involved as investors in this initial campaign of Houtmans.

00:08:43.013 --> 00:08:43.947
 Three ships.

00:08:44.643 --> 00:08:46.714
 Two hundred and forty-nine crew members.

00:08:50.449 --> 00:08:53.913
Nobody in Holland had ever
undertaken a voyage this far.

00:08:55.713 --> 00:08:59.394
Moreover, should they cross
into hostile territory, the Portuguese

00:08:59.406 --> 00:09:03.319
could at any moment intercept the
expedition, and destroy their ships.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:08.598
Plagued with scurvy and
rebellions, the voyage did not go well,

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:12.164
and Houtman had to stop over
for several months in Madagascar.

00:09:13.455 --> 00:09:15.104
Before setting sail for
Asia, he was forced to

00:09:15.116 --> 00:09:16.918
leave the country. And
ultimately reaching Bantam,

00:09:17.903 --> 00:09:19.142
 on the island of Java.

00:09:27.037 --> 00:09:28.463
 Nothing was left to chance.

00:09:29.465 --> 00:09:32.224
During his stay in Lisbon,
Houtman realized that this was

00:09:32.236 --> 00:09:34.864
somewhere where he'd be
able to buy pepper and spices.

00:09:38.887 --> 00:09:42.589
But when he reached his destination,
he was met with disappointment.

00:09:45.101 --> 00:09:48.514
The Sultan, who initially greeted
him warmly, took him prisoner.

00:09:59.176 --> 00:10:00.449
 Houtman escaped,

00:10:00.874 --> 00:10:04.881
and after several journeys,
on August the fourteenth, 1597,

00:10:05.577 --> 00:10:07.156
 he returned to Amsterdam.

00:10:11.196 --> 00:10:14.750
More than two years later,
about eighty of the original two

00:10:14.762 --> 00:10:18.565
hundred and some people make
it back. They look like skeletons.

00:10:19.074 --> 00:10:23.641
And they've got just a few, really, a few
sacks of pepper and spices in their hold.

00:10:24.083 --> 00:10:28.140
So by any logical means,
this is a complete disaster,

00:10:30.246 --> 00:10:32.775
except that handful of
men who backed the voyage

00:10:33.947 --> 00:10:37.953
realized they had done it. They had actually
gone the whole way there, somehow or other

00:10:37.965 --> 00:10:42.028
gotten their hands on some of the spices and
came back. So they'd beaten the Portuguese.

00:10:42.453 --> 00:10:43.930
 And they realized then

00:10:44.456 --> 00:10:47.886
that this was an opportunity.
This was going to work.

00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:59.491
Amsterdam now needed to gather
enough resources that it would need for its

00:10:59.503 --> 00:11:04.134
global ambitions. It needed ships.
Lots of ships. And as quickly as possible.

00:11:07.597 --> 00:11:11.895
And it just so happened that, in
1594, that is to say three years

00:11:11.907 --> 00:11:16.086
before Houtman's return to
Amsterdam, one man found the answer.

00:11:18.429 --> 00:11:21.519
His name was Cornelis
Corneliszoon, and he owned several

00:11:21.531 --> 00:11:24.252
mills in the village of
Oudgeest, near Amsterdam.

00:11:29.023 --> 00:11:31.349
Cornelius Corneliszoon
was a great inventor.

00:11:32.538 --> 00:11:34.830
 He helped Holland harness wind power.

00:11:40.466 --> 00:11:45.254
Until this point, sawing planks of wood
required several men and lots of patience.

00:11:49.651 --> 00:11:53.607
But this all changed when Cornelis
Corneliszoon developed the crankshaft.

00:11:54.796 --> 00:11:57.241
This revolutionized
shipbuilding production.

00:12:02.877 --> 00:12:06.728
The crankshaft is actually
a very simple invention that

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:10.534
converts a circular movement
into a vertical movement.

00:12:18.887 --> 00:12:23.005
With this new technique, it was
possible to produce planks of wood,

00:12:23.017 --> 00:12:27.512
which were mostly used for shipbuilding,
thirty times faster than before.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:33.480
This considerably lowered production costs
and sped up the shipbuilding process, which

00:12:33.504 --> 00:12:39.285
made the production of ships, large ships, much
cheaper. And you have to remember, this was

00:12:39.297 --> 00:12:44.643
happening at the end of the sixteenth century.
The patent dates back to around 1592 to

00:12:44.667 --> 00:12:50.517
1594. In other words, exactly at the time when
the Dutch set off on their huge explorations.

00:12:55.458 --> 00:12:58.146
At first, the invention
met a lot of opposition, and

00:12:58.158 --> 00:13:01.010
many shipyard workers
refused this piece of innovation.

00:13:06.528 --> 00:13:10.180
But within a few years, its usage
became more widespread, and

00:13:10.192 --> 00:13:14.151
Holland's advantage over its
foreign competitors was overwhelming.

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:23.811
Amsterdam was now the
leading shipyard in Europe,

00:13:25.203 --> 00:13:28.412
and that's where they mass-produced
the best trade ship of its time.

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:37.563
The five years that followed
were full of excitement.

00:13:38.565 --> 00:13:43.200
More and more expeditions were launched,
and many shipping companies were founded.

00:13:44.626 --> 00:13:46.663
 Seventy-five ships were sent to Asia.

00:13:53.421 --> 00:13:57.818
And the ships start coming back loaded
with these incredibly valuable spices.

00:13:58.242 --> 00:14:00.908
And this is really the moment
that the Dutch Golden Age takes off.

00:14:07.088 --> 00:14:09.685
There's a scene that's described
by several writers where,

00:14:10.229 --> 00:14:11.672
 when these first ships come back,

00:14:12.147 --> 00:14:15.149
all the church bells are
ringing, because this is just

00:14:15.161 --> 00:14:18.174
this awareness that something
really big has happened.

00:14:21.926 --> 00:14:25.407
These expeditions brought
back nine million Dutch guilders,

00:14:25.419 --> 00:14:28.853
which equated to the total
sum of Holland's national debt.

00:14:29.736 --> 00:14:32.147
The debt amassed during
the war against Spain.

00:14:38.005 --> 00:14:44.400
They couldn't carry on this way forever, because all these
companies would eventually start competing. They all wanted to

00:14:44.424 --> 00:14:51.353
travel to Asia and buy spices. The prices in Asia started climbing,
which nobody was happy about. And the second problem was the

00:14:51.365 --> 00:14:57.659
Portuguese and Spanish, who didn't want to relinquish their
spoils to the Dutch. They needed to be a well-organized and

00:14:57.683 --> 00:15:04.779
centralized company, with a monopoly of the market, and one that
would be able to defend itself against the Spanish and Portuguese.

00:15:05.407 --> 00:15:12.470
 And so, VOC was created.

00:15:23.234 --> 00:15:27.292
It's a key date in the history of
capitalism, as well as that of Amsterdam.

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:35.917
One morning in April 1602, Dirk van Oss
transformed a room of his house into a temporary

00:15:35.929 --> 00:15:41.451
office for the Dutch East India Company, of
which he'd just been nominated a director.

00:15:43.658 --> 00:15:46.867
Whoever wished to could buy
shares of this new company.

00:15:49.295 --> 00:15:54.389
There was this sense among the Dutch that
whoever you were, you could have a piece

00:15:54.401 --> 00:15:59.753
of the action. You could be involved in this
great economic activity of your society.

00:16:02.894 --> 00:16:06.544
One thousand one hundred and
forty-three people seized this opportunity.

00:16:09.363 --> 00:16:13.453
Not just traders, but also joiners,
bronze smiths, and all sorts

00:16:13.465 --> 00:16:17.631
of craftspeople, and even seven
people who worked as housekeepers.

00:16:19.719 --> 00:16:22.606
It was a sample of society
in Amsterdam at that time.

00:16:24.881 --> 00:16:27.638
A total of three million six
hundred and eighty thousand

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.517
Dutch guilders were invested
in this new shipping company,

00:16:31.027 --> 00:16:35.543
a huge success for the
first operation of its kind in

00:16:36.001 --> 00:16:41.922
Dirk van Oss and his house in this moment when these
ordinary people came walking into his house to buy shares

00:16:41.934 --> 00:16:47.971
of stock might have an outsized place in history, because this
is, you could say, the moment that capitalism was born.

00:16:55.016 --> 00:17:00.182
But there was a problem. The investors
were used to investing in a single voyage.

00:17:00.206 --> 00:17:05.373
They had to wait. And when the ships
returned, they put their money back, and could

00:17:05.385 --> 00:17:10.401
either choose to continue or stop investing.
But with the VOC in 1602, they were

00:17:10.425 --> 00:17:15.441
told, you can invest, but for ten years.
Your money will be held for ten years.

00:17:18.174 --> 00:17:21.349
And ten years was much too
long for many small investors.

00:17:23.353 --> 00:17:26.324
The VOC found the
solution on a small bridge,

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:30.976
which can now be found outside
the exit of Amsterdam station.

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:38.865
This area, in the early seventeenth
century, was home to the city's

00:17:38.877 --> 00:17:42.640
port and its many ships, which
were ready to set sail out to sea.

00:17:45.016 --> 00:17:48.720
On the old bridge, the one nearest
the sea, those who wanted to sell

00:17:48.732 --> 00:17:52.232
their shares of the VOC would
meet those who wanted to buy them.

00:17:58.259 --> 00:18:00.942
But the weather in Amsterdam
wasn't always accommodating,

00:18:01.808 --> 00:18:05.526
and on rainy days, people would
meet inside the Saint Olaf Church,

00:18:06.392 --> 00:18:10.267
a Catholic church that had been
abandoned during the Protestant victory and

00:18:10.279 --> 00:18:14.371
since left to the traders of Amsterdam,
who'd come from Scandinavian countries.

00:18:18.684 --> 00:18:21.315
This place, to some
extent, became the first

00:18:21.327 --> 00:18:23.794
stock exchange to
exist in human history.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:30.042
 The process was

00:18:30.466 --> 00:18:37.512
you did your exchange on the bridge or in the church.
And then you walked to the headquarters of the Dutch East

00:18:37.524 --> 00:18:44.456
India Company. And you had to get signatures and stamps.
And so that sealed the deal. That made it efficient.

00:18:47.818 --> 00:18:50.044
The Dutch East India
Company headquarters

00:18:50.056 --> 00:18:52.453
are now part of the
University of Amsterdam.

00:18:56.494 --> 00:18:59.144
It was the first headquarters
in the world, with

00:18:59.156 --> 00:19:01.655
its offices, managers
and board of directors.

00:19:06.222 --> 00:19:11.061
The edifice is the work of Hendrik de
Keizer, the leading architect at that time.

00:19:12.860 --> 00:19:16.017
He's the one they called
when as stock trading activity

00:19:16.029 --> 00:19:19.312
increased, they decided to
build a proper stock exchange.

00:19:23.200 --> 00:19:25.713
Hendrik de Keizer had
two models to work with,

00:19:27.529 --> 00:19:29.889
 the stock exchange in Antwerp and its copy,

00:19:31.926 --> 00:19:33.387
 the stock exchange in London.

00:19:35.101 --> 00:19:38.870
But Antwerp was in enemy territory,
so Hendrik de Keizer went to England.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:44.745
Here only merchandise was traded,
company shares did not yet exist.

00:19:47.122 --> 00:19:48.955
 But the architecture was interesting,

00:19:49.465 --> 00:19:52.028
and Hendrik de Keizer
used it for inspiration.

00:19:54.847 --> 00:19:58.565
Once back in Amsterdam, he built
an edifice based on the same model.

00:20:00.891 --> 00:20:04.427
Its location is no coincidence.
The new stock exchange was built

00:20:04.439 --> 00:20:08.259
right in the center of Amsterdam,
a stone's throw from the city hall,

00:20:10.178 --> 00:20:13.200
a sign that finance and politics
would work hand in hand.

00:20:16.477 --> 00:20:20.636
In Amsterdam, unlike London,
merchandise was being exchanged,

00:20:21.162 --> 00:20:23.471
but so were shares of the
Dutch East India Company.

00:20:25.033 --> 00:20:27.661
In this regard, the Amsterdam
Stock Exchange became

00:20:27.673 --> 00:20:30.008
the first in the history
of global economics.

00:20:37.495 --> 00:20:40.653
The stock exchange was
inaugurated in 1611, meanwhile a

00:20:40.665 --> 00:20:44.117
truce was being signed between
the Dutch Republic and Spain.

00:20:46.409 --> 00:20:49.114
Temporarily relieved of
the burden of its war debts,

00:20:49.126 --> 00:20:51.842
the city would finally have
the resources to expand.

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:03.641
In the space of thirty years, ten
square kilometers were dug up, around

00:21:03.653 --> 00:21:07.869
a hundred bridges were constructed,
and three thousand houses were built.

00:21:11.146 --> 00:21:15.431
With thirty thousand inhabitants in 1585,
and a hundred and five thousand thirty

00:21:15.443 --> 00:21:20.059
years later, Amsterdam would soon be the
third largest city on the European continent.

00:21:33.183 --> 00:21:35.696
The next part of this
story took place in London,

00:21:36.239 --> 00:21:37.546
 at the heart of the city,

00:21:37.954 --> 00:21:39.159
 the business district.

00:21:40.925 --> 00:21:44.252
Back then, much like now,
it was a city of traders too.

00:21:47.342 --> 00:21:51.910
In the early seventeenth century, London
had not yet undergone its urban revolution.

00:21:53.590 --> 00:21:55.662
 It was still a city in the Middle Ages.

00:21:58.633 --> 00:22:02.979
Well, it was actually a very small
place compared to London we know now.

00:22:07.207 --> 00:22:10.298
A tiny fraction of the
scale of the city as it

00:22:10.310 --> 00:22:13.743
exists in the XXI century,
even in the XIX century.

00:22:17.988 --> 00:22:20.772
The city is currently
a crucial financial hub,

00:22:21.468 --> 00:22:22.826
 and not many people live there.

00:22:24.626 --> 00:22:26.646
But back at this time
in history, most of the

00:22:26.658 --> 00:22:28.955
population of London
were still mostly living here.

00:22:31.298 --> 00:22:36.448
And the city was the area confined to the
old medieval walls, which had been built on

00:22:36.460 --> 00:22:41.621
top of the old Roman walls. So really a very
small space, what we call a square mile.

00:22:50.195 --> 00:22:53.529
A place that was made
up of medieval houses,

00:22:53.541 --> 00:22:57.258
packed very closely
together, essentially timber.

00:22:57.682 --> 00:23:01.142
There are some new
structures amongst them, but it

00:23:01.154 --> 00:23:04.626
was very much a medieval
city, very old-fashioned.

00:23:09.431 --> 00:23:11.774
 The city was separate from the royal power.

00:23:13.607 --> 00:23:16.464
The sovereigns needed money
lent to them by traders, who

00:23:16.476 --> 00:23:19.244
would, in return, receive a
certain degree of freedom.

00:23:21.926 --> 00:23:24.168
England's great fortunes
had their home there.

00:23:29.295 --> 00:23:32.232
In 1534, when Henry
VIII split from the Roman

00:23:32.244 --> 00:23:35.322
Church, he seized all
the Catholic monasteries.

00:23:39.023 --> 00:23:43.030
Until this point, they occupied
sixty percent of the city's space.

00:23:45.509 --> 00:23:48.293
The king redistributed
this among his courtiers.

00:23:55.050 --> 00:23:59.074
And so when the king
appropriates that wealth for himself,

00:23:59.702 --> 00:24:01.264
 you have a change in ownership

00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:06.236
of the property of more than
half of the buildings in the city of

00:24:06.248 --> 00:24:11.112
London. And they move from conservative
landlords, who are the churchmen,

00:24:11.587 --> 00:24:15.404
to go-ahead entrepreneurs,
the merchants, who

00:24:15.416 --> 00:24:19.329
are buying the church
property as investments.

00:24:22.860 --> 00:24:34.192
In a period of maybe fifty years, London becomes a place which
is actually filled with extremely wealthy people living in their

00:24:34.204 --> 00:24:45.814
own very large houses. And these people spent the winter months
in London with their wives, and their wives wanted to go shopping.

00:24:46.629 --> 00:24:53.171
And shopping is absolutely the key to what
happens to London. It becomes this extraordinary

00:24:53.183 --> 00:24:59.380
emporium where people come and buy and sell,
and buy and sell high-value luxury goods.

00:25:05.305 --> 00:25:08.224
At the start of the seventeenth
century, the city of London

00:25:08.236 --> 00:25:10.874
was therefore a rapidly
developing commercial center.

00:25:12.334 --> 00:25:14.388
 But it was still far behind Amsterdam.

00:25:16.426 --> 00:25:18.465
And when the two cities
came up against each

00:25:18.477 --> 00:25:20.619
other, the city of London
would come up short.

00:25:27.478 --> 00:25:29.988
Back then, much like now,
in the city, business people

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.521
often have confidential
information disclosed to them.

00:25:35.407 --> 00:25:39.821
Especially in pubs, where eavesdroppers
would often overhear conversations.

00:25:42.215 --> 00:25:48.155
Pubs are places where people meet. Pubs
are the social hubs, and business centers

00:25:48.167 --> 00:25:54.337
need social hubs as much as residential
areas. Work is primarily a social activity.

00:25:59.465 --> 00:26:01.779
The British, unlike
most other nationalities,

00:26:01.791 --> 00:26:04.015
drink standing up
rather than sitting down.

00:26:04.932 --> 00:26:10.540
If you're sitting at a table in a French cafe, all you
hear is the gossip of your friends. You can never quite

00:26:10.552 --> 00:26:16.324
hear what's being said on the next table, which is always
far more interesting than what your friends are saying.

00:26:17.682 --> 00:26:19.753
 In a British pub, no such problem.

00:26:20.161 --> 00:26:24.363
Because you're standing up, you can easily get
close enough to the other group to hear what they're

00:26:24.375 --> 00:26:28.463
saying, and you can switch off from what your
friends are saying, which you already know anyway.

00:26:30.297 --> 00:26:33.319
Strategic information could
fall into the wrong hands.

00:26:34.422 --> 00:26:37.580
Which is exactly what
happened in late summer of 1608.

00:26:38.803 --> 00:26:40.534
 And this changed the fate of London,

00:26:41.095 --> 00:26:41.842
 Amsterdam,

00:26:42.945 --> 00:26:44.439
 and even the whole of America.

00:26:50.059 --> 00:26:54.008
That morning, a certain Henry
Hudson had a meeting in the offices of

00:26:54.020 --> 00:26:58.039
the Muscovy Company, in a little
street of the city called Budge Row.

00:27:01.723 --> 00:27:04.643
The Muscovy Company was
specialised in trading with Russia.

00:27:06.561 --> 00:27:09.923
And it's not the first time Henry
Hudson had put himself in their service.

00:27:11.112 --> 00:27:14.023
Henry Hudson had spent
many years undertaking

00:27:14.035 --> 00:27:17.275
many voyages to try to
find a short route to Asia.

00:27:18.531 --> 00:27:21.468
On two occasions, Hudson
tried to find the right route.

00:27:22.334 --> 00:27:24.134
 The first one went via the North Pole,

00:27:24.983 --> 00:27:26.205
 which obviously didn't work.

00:27:28.157 --> 00:27:30.530
And the second attempt
went via the far north

00:27:30.542 --> 00:27:32.979
coast of Russia, and
wasn't successful either.

00:27:36.884 --> 00:27:38.633
 I think of him as kind of like a,

00:27:39.142 --> 00:27:42.521
I don't know, a Steve Jobs or
someone like that of his era, who

00:27:43.013 --> 00:27:47.325
people started to look to
him as someone who knows.

00:27:47.733 --> 00:27:53.514
Whether or not he did know, and we know today that he
did not, but what you had to do was you had to guess.

00:27:53.526 --> 00:27:59.533
You had to say, I'm going to put all this information
together. And my next guess is that this is the best way.

00:28:00.670 --> 00:28:02.657
 This time, Hudson had a new idea.

00:28:03.658 --> 00:28:05.834
Instead of travelling
towards the east, they'd

00:28:05.846 --> 00:28:07.988
have to cross the
Atlantic and the continent.

00:28:09.787 --> 00:28:11.926
That's what he was
explaining to his employers.

00:28:14.202 --> 00:28:17.342
However, inside the building,
nothing happened as he'd expected.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:20.755
Not only did they refuse his offer,
but they showed him to the door.

00:28:26.205 --> 00:28:28.786
That said, this incident
didn't go unnoticed.

00:28:30.382 --> 00:28:35.322
A sixty-two-year-old man, Emmanuel van
Metteren, quickly seized the opportunity.

00:28:38.225 --> 00:28:42.487
As the Dutch Consul in London,
he'd look for every piece of information.

00:28:43.234 --> 00:28:49.531
And he had spies. He paid people to find out
information. And one of the things that he was following

00:28:49.543 --> 00:28:55.543
was Henry Hudson. And he found out very quickly
that the Muscovy Company had fired Henry Hudson.

00:28:56.222 --> 00:28:57.139
 And so he

00:28:57.750 --> 00:28:59.006
 approached him and said,

00:28:59.787 --> 00:29:00.772
 come to Amsterdam.

00:29:01.196 --> 00:29:01.893
 We'll fund you.

00:29:03.285 --> 00:29:05.967
London had just missed
an amazing opportunity,

00:29:05.979 --> 00:29:08.157
because Hudson
was quickly persuaded.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:11.825
 And a month later,

00:29:12.470 --> 00:29:13.760
 he sailed to Amsterdam.

00:29:20.976 --> 00:29:24.708
He comes to Amsterdam,
and there are different accents.

00:29:24.720 --> 00:29:28.463
There are different languages.
There's different dress.

00:29:35.203 --> 00:29:43.033
It's the very moment when they are building their first stock
exchange and starting the idea of expanding this canal grid.

00:29:43.045 --> 00:29:50.823
So it's all just happening. And this multi-ethnic polyglot
society is something that would have been very strange to him.

00:29:54.286 --> 00:29:57.873
Hudson discovered a new
world. A mixed population

00:29:57.885 --> 00:30:01.196
with many refugees,
unlike his city of birth.

00:30:03.166 --> 00:30:03.692
 In Europe,

00:30:04.728 --> 00:30:06.850
 intolerance is official policy. And

00:30:07.325 --> 00:30:08.616
 if you're in London,

00:30:09.329 --> 00:30:14.745
basically you're English. This is still
that moment when that kind of identity

00:30:15.322 --> 00:30:18.106
 is intensified and

00:30:18.701 --> 00:30:20.500
 codified by law almost.

00:30:22.283 --> 00:30:27.444
Here, there was no king or absolute power,
but a republic formed of seven provinces.

00:30:28.259 --> 00:30:30.976
They all had to reach a
compromise for any big decision.

00:30:33.998 --> 00:30:36.805
When the city became
Protestant, the Catholic churches

00:30:36.817 --> 00:30:39.431
were converted into the
general places of worship.

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:44.081
But there were still
many Catholics in the city.

00:30:44.093 --> 00:30:46.358
They represented a
third of the population,

00:30:46.782 --> 00:30:48.548
 and their faith had not been banned.

00:30:52.147 --> 00:30:54.151
Their churches were
simply no longer visible.

00:30:56.511 --> 00:30:58.599
 They were now in individual homes.

00:31:01.247 --> 00:31:04.762
There were sixty-six churches like
this during the Dutch Golden Age.

00:31:07.393 --> 00:31:11.655
Everyone knew that services were held
every Sunday, but they were tolerated.

00:31:14.456 --> 00:31:17.343
At a time where Europeans
were killing each other for

00:31:17.355 --> 00:31:20.415
religious reasons, this was
proof of a greater openness.

00:31:23.607 --> 00:31:25.882
 And it's a legacy that Amsterdam passed on,

00:31:26.561 --> 00:31:27.886
 throughout the Golden Age,

00:31:28.735 --> 00:31:30.246
 across the entire world.

00:31:33.217 --> 00:31:40.721
So Hudson goes into the courtyard of
this now legendary building, which was the

00:31:41.129 --> 00:31:44.083
headquarters of the East India
Company, meets with the directors.

00:31:45.016 --> 00:31:49.957
And they hammer out an agreement
by which they will fund his voyage.

00:31:51.621 --> 00:31:53.064
 On one condition, however.

00:31:53.913 --> 00:31:56.691
Hudson would not head
west, as he'd proposed,

00:31:56.703 --> 00:31:59.736
but would find a shortcut
to Asia through Russia.

00:32:02.809 --> 00:32:07.008
They, for their own reasons, insisted
that he try a northeast passage.

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:10.993
He had tried that and failed. He
was convinced that wouldn't work.

00:32:12.741 --> 00:32:15.198
But from one line in the
contract, we know that

00:32:15.210 --> 00:32:17.937
there was tension. And
the line says something like,

00:32:19.057 --> 00:32:23.166
Hudson will think of
taking no other route than

00:32:23.709 --> 00:32:24.490
 a northeast

00:32:24.932 --> 00:32:25.475
 passage.

00:32:26.035 --> 00:32:28.808
Which suggests that he was saying, no,
no, no. We want to go northwest. We want

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:31.604
to go northwest. And they were saying,
no, no, no. We want you to do northeast.

00:32:34.932 --> 00:32:38.392
In May 1609, as he set
sail for Russia, the seafarer

00:32:38.404 --> 00:32:41.876
was under no illusions as
to his chances of success.

00:32:44.524 --> 00:32:46.341
 But he had other intentions.

00:32:48.769 --> 00:32:52.741
Having only just arrived in Norway, and
facing the difficulties they'd encountered,

00:32:53.183 --> 00:32:55.577
 he persuaded his crew to do a U-turn,

00:32:57.631 --> 00:32:59.176
 and to head towards America.

00:33:02.674 --> 00:33:05.732
The Indians, so we're told,
mentioned a waterway that

00:33:05.744 --> 00:33:08.701
wound its way far into
the depths of the continent.

00:33:09.923 --> 00:33:11.960
 Hudson looked for it along the north side,

00:33:12.572 --> 00:33:13.455
 turned round,

00:33:14.575 --> 00:33:16.341
 and finally found the river Mouth.

00:33:18.616 --> 00:33:21.689
He made his way down this river,
which would later take his name,

00:33:23.200 --> 00:33:24.117
 the Hudson River.

00:33:24.983 --> 00:33:28.446
But after a few days of sailing, he
had to face facts. It was a dead end.

00:33:32.164 --> 00:33:33.930
 So Hudson turned back on himself.

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:37.954
 Along the route, he met some Indian tribes,

00:33:39.193 --> 00:33:41.689
 some hostile, others happy to trade.

00:33:44.320 --> 00:33:48.378
And he saw lots of beavers, whose
fur was worth a fortune in Europe.

00:33:53.845 --> 00:33:56.901
Amsterdam had not found the
sea route it had been looking for.

00:33:58.497 --> 00:34:01.553
But the city had set foot
on the American continent.

00:34:12.521 --> 00:34:17.495
The year Hudson returned was also the year
Amsterdam underwent its transformation.

00:34:21.264 --> 00:34:25.844
Amsterdam, until that time, was
still this little medieval city. But

00:34:25.856 --> 00:34:30.382
from that moment, they undertake
this vast urban expansion program.

00:34:33.455 --> 00:34:37.699
A few years earlier, a new district
had popped up in the northeast of

00:34:38.633 --> 00:34:40.908
Many refugees from
Antwerp had settled there.

00:34:44.235 --> 00:34:47.232
The less wealthy were living
on top of each other in the three

00:34:47.244 --> 00:34:50.348
thousand three hundred small
homes built outside the city walls.

00:34:51.230 --> 00:34:55.243
They lay out a plan for a
belt of canals, a kind of U of

00:34:55.255 --> 00:34:59.634
canals that will wrap around
the medieval center of the city.

00:35:00.110 --> 00:35:02.198
 And the canals will be used

00:35:02.758 --> 00:35:05.713
 to take the problem of water and convert it

00:35:06.188 --> 00:35:07.461
 to an advantage.

00:35:10.042 --> 00:35:13.081
A team was put together to
carry out this ambitious project,

00:35:13.709 --> 00:35:16.273
and Hendrik de Keizer was
one of the men in charge.

00:35:20.772 --> 00:35:24.188
At the start of the seventeenth
century, this construction

00:35:24.200 --> 00:35:27.512
company was formed of
around a hundred and fifty people.

00:35:28.157 --> 00:35:34.980
Specialists, craftspeople from various fields, masons,
carpenters, sculptors, and this number just kept

00:35:34.992 --> 00:35:42.419
growing. And we know that in the mid-seventeenth century,
it had between six hundred to seven hundred employees.

00:35:44.066 --> 00:35:47.954
There were huge difficulties, because
Amsterdam is built on marshy land,

00:35:48.412 --> 00:35:50.874
whereas each building
needs a solid foundation.

00:35:54.575 --> 00:35:58.559
As such, hundreds of ships traveled
back and forth to the Swedish port

00:35:58.571 --> 00:36:02.623
of Stavanger, to bring dozens of
thousands of tree trunks to Amsterdam.

00:36:07.580 --> 00:36:11.381
Amsterdam was transforming itself
into a huge, bristling construction

00:36:11.393 --> 00:36:15.424
site of wooden triangles, on top of
which heavy loads were being hoisted.

00:36:16.511 --> 00:36:19.540
And so, this is how the posts
necessary for the foundation

00:36:19.552 --> 00:36:22.130
of such construction were
buried into the ground.

00:36:22.691 --> 00:36:25.118
 One post per meter across the entire city.

00:36:29.685 --> 00:36:32.826
 So I have often thought that if you were

00:36:33.726 --> 00:36:34.796
 walking through

00:36:35.475 --> 00:36:37.139
 Amsterdam in its golden age,

00:36:37.818 --> 00:36:44.972
I think really you would have been struck by the sound of
hammering and of the piles being driven and driven, because that

00:36:44.984 --> 00:36:52.266
was going on all through the century as this belt of canals was
dug and these roads and bridges and houses were being built.

00:36:53.641 --> 00:36:55.967
The perimeter of the
city wall was extended.

00:36:56.477 --> 00:37:00.415
The refugee homes were demolished,
and two new districts emerged.

00:37:05.203 --> 00:37:06.511
 A residential district,

00:37:07.342 --> 00:37:09.264
and on the outside
was a commercial and

00:37:09.276 --> 00:37:11.451
residential area for
those with fewer means.

00:37:13.556 --> 00:37:16.986
It was the first town planning scheme
in the history of European cities.

00:37:21.621 --> 00:37:24.592
In the commercial area, each
profession had its own area.

00:37:31.078 --> 00:37:34.028
Whereas in the residential
areas, it was forbidden to make

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:36.952
noise, and no polluting forms
of industry were tolerated.

00:37:41.383 --> 00:37:43.903
Here, everybody was free
to build the house of their

00:37:43.915 --> 00:37:46.256
choice, to the size and
style of their pleasing.

00:37:50.704 --> 00:37:51.960
 There was only one restriction.

00:37:53.522 --> 00:37:56.935
The house could only occupy
half the surface of the plot of land.

00:38:02.113 --> 00:38:05.087
And what's unique about
the houses in Amsterdam

00:38:05.099 --> 00:38:08.395
along the canals, is that
each of them has a garden.

00:38:17.546 --> 00:38:23.888
The inhabitants of Amsterdam who lived in these
houses, the wealthy traders, the bankers and men in

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:30.127
power, were passionate gardeners, and wanted to
create palatial gardens at the heart of the city.

00:38:31.757 --> 00:38:34.476
And the Dutch way of
designing these urban gardens

00:38:34.488 --> 00:38:36.952
was characterized by
their different designs.

00:38:38.633 --> 00:38:44.439
There were boxwood hedges,
plant beds, topiary, original plants.

00:38:45.390 --> 00:38:49.074
There were also statues,
benches, garden pavilions.

00:38:50.008 --> 00:38:52.983
Everything you'd find in
a palace garden could be

00:38:52.995 --> 00:38:56.341
found here, but on a smaller
scale, in a smaller space.

00:39:07.648 --> 00:39:10.229
On the ground floor of the
house, there'd be a reception area.

00:39:14.015 --> 00:39:16.295
Next door would be
the office, where one's

00:39:16.307 --> 00:39:18.972
business would be run,
with one or two employees.

00:39:22.996 --> 00:39:24.779
 Upstairs was the family space.

00:39:25.747 --> 00:39:29.074
Family in the strict sense of
the term, as we know it today.

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:36.918
Before that, European homes
tended to be much more mixed.

00:39:37.359 --> 00:39:40.857
You may have extended
family. There might be servants or

00:39:41.264 --> 00:39:43.268
 boarders who were renting rooms.

00:39:45.033 --> 00:39:51.417
The Dutch canal house, and this again reinforces this new
notion of what an individual is, and not just an individual,

00:39:51.429 --> 00:39:58.039
but what a family is. This new kind of sensibility, which I
think most of us consider now, today, to be part of our lives.

00:40:01.366 --> 00:40:02.623
 The Dutch canal house

00:40:03.064 --> 00:40:06.069
was intended to be for a man
and his wife and their children.

00:40:10.993 --> 00:40:14.615
The Dutch have this
word, gezelligheid. It

00:40:14.627 --> 00:40:18.514
means cozy, intimate,
a place of domesticity.

00:40:26.001 --> 00:40:29.265
When you read the tales
of foreigners who walked

00:40:29.277 --> 00:40:32.487
along the canals in the
mid-eighteenth century,

00:40:33.522 --> 00:40:38.005
they seem surprised that there
are so many high-value houses,

00:40:38.480 --> 00:40:39.295
 and no sovereign.

00:40:40.942 --> 00:40:46.053
They are also surprised that this
architecture is not solely used for the houses

00:40:46.065 --> 00:40:51.315
of rich individuals, but that even simple
public buildings are built this way too.

00:40:53.268 --> 00:40:56.154
 Orphanages, retirement homes.

00:40:59.787 --> 00:41:04.511
They thought it's really quite
astonishing, and they even thought

00:41:04.523 --> 00:41:08.972
it's a bit excessive to design
public buildings like palaces.

00:41:14.473 --> 00:41:17.306
Amsterdam would now start
exporting this revolutionary

00:41:17.318 --> 00:41:19.957
societal design to the
other side of the Atlantic.

00:41:27.275 --> 00:41:30.614
1623 marked the creation of
the Dutch West India Company,

00:41:30.626 --> 00:41:33.573
which had a monopoly
over trade with the Americas.

00:41:36.035 --> 00:41:38.598
A year later, it invited
volunteers to its

00:41:38.610 --> 00:41:41.604
headquarters in Amsterdam
to join its new colony.

00:41:42.385 --> 00:41:43.573
 It's the golden age

00:41:44.032 --> 00:41:44.456
 in

00:41:44.949 --> 00:41:45.356
 Holland,

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:47.716
 and it's hard to get settlers.

00:41:48.242 --> 00:41:48.769
 So

00:41:49.363 --> 00:41:52.843
 they are advertising, and they have this

00:41:53.285 --> 00:41:56.154
 multi-ethnic society. And as

00:41:57.665 --> 00:41:58.989
 you would imagine would happen,

00:41:59.414 --> 00:42:03.132
it's people who are at the bottom
rung of the society who are willing

00:42:03.709 --> 00:42:07.478
to, they're the ones who have
nothing to lose, who say, OK, I'll join.

00:42:11.179 --> 00:42:14.286
Young people in their
twenties, a boy for each girl.

00:42:15.390 --> 00:42:18.225
All from French-speaking
Flanders, now Belgium.

00:42:20.246 --> 00:42:24.541
In 1624, a few dozen volunteers
signed up for this American adventure.

00:42:26.511 --> 00:42:29.653
At that point, Amsterdam
and the Dutch Republic were,

00:42:29.665 --> 00:42:32.877
without contest, the world's
leading commercial power.

00:42:39.651 --> 00:42:44.235
However, on the other side of the North
Sea, London was building up its forces.

00:42:45.814 --> 00:42:49.248
Its secret? Textiles. A
force born two hundred and

00:42:49.260 --> 00:42:52.504
fifty years earlier, in
terrible circumstances.

00:43:07.020 --> 00:43:09.668
 The greatest epidemic in European history.

00:43:10.704 --> 00:43:11.655
 The Black Death.

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:15.747
Half of the English population were
wiped out in the space of a decade.

00:43:17.988 --> 00:43:22.045
But it was at this point that the English
economy discovered sheep farming.

00:43:30.908 --> 00:43:33.539
Before the Black Death, there
was a very high population.

00:43:34.235 --> 00:43:41.303
The economy was geared towards agricultural produce
to feed this population. But after the Black Death,

00:43:41.315 --> 00:43:48.395
there was what they called a dearth of people. There
were very few people, and there was a lot of land.

00:43:48.989 --> 00:43:53.859
And so the economy changed, and
they made use of the huge amount of land

00:43:53.871 --> 00:43:58.820
by switching the economy from this
agricultural economy to sheep farming.

00:44:02.555 --> 00:44:08.515
Wool supplies were gathering in London, and for the next
two centuries, the city traders exported it, particularly

00:44:08.527 --> 00:44:14.032
to Amsterdam and Holland, where a great number of some
of the best weavers on the continent were working.

00:44:18.548 --> 00:44:22.838
This was all very well. Exporting
wool was very good. It was a cargo that

00:44:22.862 --> 00:44:27.450
made good profit. But there was a way
to make even more profit, and that was

00:44:27.462 --> 00:44:31.966
to not only send wool abroad to allow
people to make cloth out of it, but to

00:44:31.990 --> 00:44:36.494
make the cloth yourself and then
sell that with its increased added value.

00:44:41.281 --> 00:44:45.475
In the mid-seventeenth century, London
finally decided to take the next step.

00:44:49.295 --> 00:44:52.930
The weavers of Holland were
invited to move to the English

00:44:52.942 --> 00:44:56.341
capital, as well as French
Protestants, the Huguenots.

00:45:01.264 --> 00:45:03.455
 Among them were many textile craftsmen.

00:45:04.864 --> 00:45:07.750
London thus sowed the
seeds for its future industry.

00:45:13.641 --> 00:45:16.955
They settled in the east of
London, near to the city of London

00:45:16.967 --> 00:45:20.398
and close to the port where they
had arrived from the continent.

00:45:27.665 --> 00:45:31.858
They built houses with weaving
lofts at the top of the houses with large

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:36.018
windows and plenty of daylight to
operate their looms for making cloth.

00:45:43.573 --> 00:45:50.216
And this early development of the cloth industry
in the UK was what really allowed the economy of

00:45:50.228 --> 00:45:56.748
London to take off, which produced the great
wealth that led to it becoming a financial centre.

00:46:00.449 --> 00:46:03.353
The weavers formed the
initial hub of the future East End,

00:46:04.252 --> 00:46:07.003
the major industrial district
of the nineteenth century.

00:46:09.550 --> 00:46:12.741
And the port in London saw
its activity increase tenfold.

00:46:19.787 --> 00:46:23.183
In a space of fifty years, the
population of London more than doubled.

00:46:24.609 --> 00:46:29.006
By the mid-seventeenth century, it had
amassed five hundred thousand inhabitants.

00:46:31.027 --> 00:46:33.942
The medieval city grew
beyond its confines, and rapidly

00:46:33.954 --> 00:46:36.986
spread towards the west, in
the direction of Westminster.

00:46:39.770 --> 00:46:44.643
Westminster, where the King reigned, and
he saw urbanization sprawl beyond control.

00:46:48.225 --> 00:46:56.467
There were real anxieties about the expansion of London,
because it was this place of disease, of vice, of crime.

00:46:56.479 --> 00:47:04.371
And the idea that the city might expand, might get
bigger, was an incredibly worrying thing for that reason.

00:47:05.967 --> 00:47:09.482
In his palace in Whitehall, the
King dreamt of a different city,

00:47:11.044 --> 00:47:13.251
 a city devoted to royal greatness.

00:47:16.714 --> 00:47:19.657
The example to follow,
according to Charles I, is

00:47:19.669 --> 00:47:22.860
the Banqueting House, one
of the wings of his palace.

00:47:26.935 --> 00:47:30.874
With its classic design, the building
was used for great royal ceremonies.

00:47:32.826 --> 00:47:35.831
 It was built by his father, King James I.

00:47:40.704 --> 00:47:43.458
The King, featured in
these ceiling paintings,

00:47:43.470 --> 00:47:46.001
done by Rubens in
person, celebrate glory.

00:47:52.419 --> 00:47:54.796
The two sovereigns
made life difficult in London.

00:47:55.865 --> 00:47:59.974
By imposing all sorts of regulations,
they wanted to force it to change.

00:48:04.337 --> 00:48:05.220
 But they had a problem.

00:48:05.882 --> 00:48:11.446
Unlike European monarchies, where
the monarch genuinely had the power to

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:17.820
completely remodel city centres, James I
and Charles I were completely dependent

00:48:17.832 --> 00:48:24.030
upon Parliament. And the City of London
government had a complete stranglehold,

00:48:24.054 --> 00:48:30.093
complete control over the nature of
the city. And so their hands were tied.

00:48:31.825 --> 00:48:35.390
London became one of the bones of
contention between the King and Parliament.

00:48:36.629 --> 00:48:39.129
And the conflict contributed
to the civil war which

00:48:39.141 --> 00:48:41.604
devastated England in the
mid-seventeenth century.

00:48:43.964 --> 00:48:47.020
 Charles I was defeated, sentenced to death,

00:48:47.614 --> 00:48:52.676
and decapitated on thirtieth of January
1649, in front of the Banqueting House,

00:48:52.688 --> 00:48:57.699
the palace he'd so dearly loved, and
recommended as an example to be followed.

00:49:08.005 --> 00:49:09.312
 It was a terrible period.

00:49:10.246 --> 00:49:12.979
But the city emerged richer
and more powerful than before,

00:49:20.602 --> 00:49:23.578
because this is when the
first banks set themselves up in

00:49:23.590 --> 00:49:26.629
London, and financial business
became a formal profession.

00:49:30.059 --> 00:49:32.546
Until this point, things
had been done in an

00:49:32.558 --> 00:49:35.169
informal manner, mostly
in settings like pubs.

00:49:37.937 --> 00:49:41.994
A rich tradesman would sit at a table,
from which he'd hold business meetings.

00:49:46.511 --> 00:49:49.453
A person who wanted to borrow
money would come up to him,

00:49:49.465 --> 00:49:52.317
accompanied by another
person who'd act as a guarantee.

00:49:55.288 --> 00:49:57.594
And the rich tradesman, if
he deemed them suitable,

00:49:57.606 --> 00:49:59.567
would lend them the
money they'd requested.

00:50:01.485 --> 00:50:04.422
But this practice changed
after the death of Charles I.

00:50:11.078 --> 00:50:12.911
 A few months after his execution,

00:50:13.404 --> 00:50:15.543
 Oliver Cromwell became Lord Protector,

00:50:16.714 --> 00:50:17.886
 a sort of dictator,

00:50:18.667 --> 00:50:20.670
 and allowed Jews to return to England.

00:50:21.162 --> 00:50:23.522
They'd been deported
several centuries beforehand.

00:50:32.402 --> 00:50:36.375
As such, Jewish bankers moved
to London from Lombardy in Italy.

00:50:39.991 --> 00:50:43.896
They chose a street in the city
center, now called Lombard Street,

00:50:44.388 --> 00:50:45.780
 and set up their offices there.

00:50:53.166 --> 00:50:56.188
These were the first office blocks
such as we know them today.

00:51:01.451 --> 00:51:07.061
However, the newcomers still maintained a
vestige of the old practices. This type of sign,

00:51:07.073 --> 00:51:12.572
which all English pubs worthy of this name
would hang outside, and would be named after.

00:51:18.106 --> 00:51:22.052
Pubs and coffeehouses had done this for
a long time. They'd always had a picture

00:51:22.064 --> 00:51:26.120
outside, a picture of the king or a picture
of a castle or something more unusual.

00:51:27.869 --> 00:51:31.134
And so it is in Lombard Street
that to this day we see a collection

00:51:31.146 --> 00:51:34.422
of signs hanging from buildings
that look very much like pub signs.

00:51:38.718 --> 00:51:39.923
 They don't have any words on them.

00:51:40.331 --> 00:51:42.504
 They are the world's first logos.

00:51:45.543 --> 00:51:47.292
 So when we see the cat and fiddle

00:51:47.852 --> 00:51:51.143
over a building in Lombard
Street, this is because it was the

00:51:51.155 --> 00:51:54.405
visual symbol of the company,
the bank that operated within,

00:51:54.915 --> 00:51:58.429
very much as had been the
case in the days of pub banking.

00:52:00.806 --> 00:52:04.755
In the space of fifty years, the city
had carried out its transformation.

00:52:04.767 --> 00:52:08.140
From a commercial center, it
was now becoming a financial hub.

00:52:14.830 --> 00:52:18.802
England now had the means required
for its ambitions, and a first war soon

00:52:18.814 --> 00:52:23.064
broke out between London and Amsterdam
to take control of certain sea channels.

00:52:27.139 --> 00:52:28.870
 But Amsterdam continued to grow,

00:52:30.449 --> 00:52:33.794
an incredible success which
aroused keen interest from all around.

